error[Z0402]: `dunnett` is not one of the allowed words
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:38
  |
2 | d.anova(x, group: g).posthoc(method: dunnett)
  |                                      ^^^^^^^ expected one of tukey, bonferroni, holm, hochberg, hommel, bh, by, sidak, scheffe, none

error[Z0401]: `method` of `posthoc` must be a tukey | bonferroni | holm | hochberg | hommel | bh | by | sidak | scheffe | none, but this is an Int
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:38
  |
2 | d.anova(x, group: g).posthoc(method: 2)
  |                                      ^ an Int
  |
  = help: usage: `fit.posthoc(method: tukey | bonferroni | holm | hochberg | hommel | bh | by | sidak | scheffe | none = tukey, term: Term, level: Float = 0.95)`

error[Z0401]: `posthoc` compares the group means of an `anova`, but this is the result of `kruskal_test`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:1
  |
2 | d.kruskal_test(x, group: g).posthoc()
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ not the result of an `anova`
  |
  = help: write `d.anova(y, group: g).posthoc()`; the comparisons rest on the group means and variances of that test

error[Z0401]: `posthoc` compares the group means of an `anova`, but this is the result of `t_test`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:1
  |
2 | d.t_test(x, group: g).posthoc(method: holm)
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ not the result of an `anova`
  |
  = help: write `d.anova(y, group: g).posthoc()`; the comparisons rest on the group means and variances of that test

note: no term of the formula is a category, so each row tests a slope; `reg` gives the estimates (line 2)
error[Z0401]: `posthoc` compares the levels of a category, but no term of `y ~ a + b` is a category or an interaction of categories
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:1
  |
2 | d.anova(y ~ a + b).posthoc()
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ no category among the terms
  |
  = help: a numeric variable has a slope and no levels; `category(x)` in the formula treats its values as levels

error[Z0401]: `posthoc` cannot be called on a Frame
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:1
  |
2 | d.posthoc()
  | ^ this is a Frame
  |
  = help: `posthoc` takes a TestResult first; `x.posthoc(...)` is `posthoc(x, ...)`

error[Z0401]: `posthoc` cannot be called on a RegFit
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:1
  |
2 | d.reg(x ~ g).posthoc()
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^ this is a RegFit
  |
  = help: `posthoc` takes a TestResult first; `x.posthoc(...)` is `posthoc(x, ...)`

note: `tukey` pools the variances of the groups, which the `anova` did not assume equal; `method: holm` compares the pairs by Welch's t-tests (line 2)
error[Z0401]: `posthoc` compares the group means of an `anova`, but this is the result of `posthoc`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:1
  |
2 | d.anova(x, group: g).posthoc().posthoc()
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ not the result of an `anova`
  |
  = help: write `d.anova(y, group: g).posthoc()`; the comparisons rest on the group means and variances of that test

error[Z0401]: `level:` of `posthoc` is a confidence level between 0 and 1, but this is 1.5
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:37
  |
2 | d.anova(x, group: g).posthoc(level: 1.5)
  |                                     ^^^ outside (0, 1)
  |
  = help: write a level such as 0.95 or 0.99

error[Z0401]: `level:` of `posthoc` is a confidence level between 0 and 1, but this is 0
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:37
  |
2 | d.anova(x, group: g).posthoc(level: 0)
  |                                     ^ outside (0, 1)
  |
  = help: write a level such as 0.95 or 0.99

error[Z0401]: `level` of `posthoc` must be a Float, but this is a Str
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:37
  |
2 | d.anova(x, group: g).posthoc(level: "high")
  |                                     ^^^^^^ a Str
  |
  = help: usage: `fit.posthoc(method: tukey | bonferroni | holm | hochberg | hommel | bh | by | sidak | scheffe | none = tukey, term: Term, level: Float = 0.95)`

note: `tukey` pools the variances of the groups, which the `anova` did not assume equal; `method: holm` compares the pairs by Welch's t-tests (line 2)
error[Z0401]: `ci` of a test's result gives the intervals the test was made at, 90%, not at 95%
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:52
  |
2 | d.anova(x, group: g).posthoc(level: 0.9).ci(level: 0.95)
  |                                                    ^^^^ another level
  |
  = help: run `posthoc` with `level:` for intervals at another level; they come from the method's own distribution, which the result does not keep

error[Z0401]: `ci` of a test's result gives the intervals the test was made at, 95%, not at 90%
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:54
  |
2 | d.anova(x, group: g).posthoc(method: none).ci(level: 0.9)
  |                                                      ^^^ another level
  |
  = help: run `posthoc` with `level:` for intervals at another level; they come from the method's own distribution, which the result does not keep

error[Z0401]: Welch's `anova` needs at least two values of `x` in every group, but g=3 has one
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:9
  |
2 | d.anova(x, group: g).posthoc()
  |         ^ a group of one
  |
  = help: `equal_var: true` pools the variance across the groups and admits a group of one; or leave the group out with `where`
